					DRAIN → COMPUTE phase boundaries on the top-level FSM state lines.]
				</p>

				<h3>Accuracy</h3>
				<p>
					We found that the stereo vision system performed best at a distance of <strong>1–3 m</strong>
					from the camera setup, with a camera baseline distance of <strong>6 cm</strong>. At
					this distance, it is possible to differentiate objects in the foreground from the
					background, with bodies and objects appearing as light, monochrome silhouettes against
					a dark, noisy background. The vision system is additionally able to pick up on
					gradients for objects which are angled towards or away from the camera, showing the
					change in the object's depth across the frame.
				</p>
				<p>
					A known issue with basic block matching is that it performs poorly on monochromatic,
					untextured surfaces. Block matching assigns similar costs to every disparity within
					such a surface — every block looks identical without a-priori knowledge of where in
					the surface that block is. We ameliorate this with the simple SGM-inspired penalty
					described in <a href="#algorithm">the Algorithm section</a>, which smooths the
					disparity map by penalizing discontinuities. This 1-way smoothing creates a
					noticeable difference between the left and right edges of objects, but it
					significantly improves performance on monochrome surfaces and on larger objects
					generally.
				</p>
				<p class="todo">
					[TODO: Insert disparity-map photos at 1 m, 2 m, and 3 m distances showing the
					silhouette quality at each. Also a side-by-side of "with vs. without SGM penalty"
					on a monochrome surface (e.g. blank wall) to make the smoothing benefit visible.]
				</p>

				<h3>Resource Utilization</h3>
				<p>
					We were able to successfully compile our design with a <strong>5×5 block size and 24
					parallel SAD workers</strong>. The limiting resource on how big we could grow the
					design was <strong>ALM usage for the SAD compute units</strong>: increasing the number
					of workers grows the ALM count roughly linearly, but increasing the block size grows
					the per-worker logic <strong>exponentially with the radius of the block</strong>. A
					5×5 block was the largest we could afford while still keeping enough workers to avoid
					bottlenecking the disparity-map computation.
				</p>
				<p>
					We initially struggled with M10K block usage because we were inadvertently
					<strong>double-buffering the incoming frame data</strong>. This was an artifact of two
					separate architectural decisions: (1) we wrote the incoming pixel data into a memory
					that was fragmented row-wise, and (2) we stored the rectified frame in that same
					memory. As a result we were initially using two separate frame memories — a monolithic
					on-chip SRAM written by the Video-In subsystem, plus the segmented row-banked memory
					that the SAD compute units read in parallel — and copying rectified pixels between
					them.
				</p>
				<p>
					To resolve this redundancy we wrote our own memory block exposing two interfaces: an
					Avalon MMIO interface to be written to by Video-In, and a parallel asynchronous
					interface to be read by the compute units. This effectively <strong>halved our M10K
					utilization</strong> and let us dedicate one M10K per row of each image — the
					condition for maximum read parallelism. The custom memory block costs about
					<strong>13,268 ALMs</strong> on its own because the MMIO interface has to mux across
					200 separate block RAMs spread physically across the die, and that fan-in is also
					what gives us our very long Quartus compile times — the fitter struggles with the
					routing.
				</p>
				<p class="todo">
					[TODO: Insert final Quartus fitter report — exact ALMs / M10Ks / DSPs / registers /
					Fmax — once the last working SOF is rebuilt. Ideally a screenshot of the resource
					summary panel.]
				</p>

				<h3>Safety and Usability</h3>
				<p>
					The system runs entirely on low-voltage lab hardware — the DE1-SoC board plus two
					analog cameras and a VGA monitor — with no actuators, RF transmitters, lasers, or
					human-connected electronics. All parameter tuning is done over SSH through the REPL,
					and the disparity map is rendered live to the VGA output so anyone in the room can
					see whether a parameter change helped or hurt.
				</p>
			</section>

			{/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */}
			<section id="conclusions">
				<h2>Conclusions</h2>
				<p>
					The end-to-end stereo-vision pipeline met our top-line goal: a real-time disparity
					map computed entirely on the DE1-SoC, with the FPGA doing all of the load-bearing
					work and the ARM HPS reduced to a thin parameter-tuning shell. The 24-way parallel
					SAD engine ran far above the camera framerate when isolated (~215 FPS at 50 MHz), and
					the in-place radial undistortion pipeline successfully gave the matcher horizontal
					epipolar lines without needing a second frame's worth of on-chip RAM.
				</p>
				<p>
					What we would do differently next time falls into two architectural decisions that
					over-constrained the design from the start. <strong>First: storing an entire frame of
					video in memory and then exploiting row parallelism for matching.</strong> Lines of
					video are received sequentially, so a leaner approach would have been a small line
					buffer of the most recent horizontal lines, with parallelism spent across disparity
					sweeps rather than across rows. We could have hit the same compute throughput with
					substantially less M10K and ALM budget — and the savings would have unblocked the
					things we left on the table (deeper SGM sweeps, the 2D <code>(d, v<sub>y</sub>)</code>
					search, a larger block size).
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Second: the rectification pipeline was the single largest bottleneck on
					throughput</strong> — the un-pipelined rectification took ~90 ms per frame, against
					only ~4 ms for the disparity computation itself. We hadn't anticipated rectification
					would be necessary at all when the architecture was set, so we never gave it a
					first-class design pass. The cleanest implementation would have been to modify pixel
					coordinates in-stream against a lookup table, but two issues steered us away: the
					reverse mapping from rectified back to raw coordinates is awkward, and an explicit
					LUT large enough for the full mapping doesn't fit on chip alongside the SAD engine.
					Knowing rectification would dominate the runtime, we would have spent more on-chip
					resources on a simpler in-stream transform rather than the multi-cycle FSM that
					currently consumes most of the frame time.
				</p>
				<h3>Intellectual Property and Standards</h3>
				<p>
					The project sits on top of the Cornell / U. Toronto <em>Computer System 15</em>
					640×480 video-mod baseline, which we forked under the course's reuse policy. The
					Video-In subsystem, VGA Pixel DMA, Lightweight HPS-to-FPGA bridge, and the Altera
					Edge Detection IP are all Intel/Altera Qsys components used unmodified. Algorithm
					prototyping and the calibration pipeline use OpenCV (Apache 2.0). Icarus Verilog and
					Pillow back the RTL image testbench. All custom RTL, C, and Python code was written
					by the project team; nothing in the design required reverse-engineering, NDAs, or
					sample parts. There are no patent encumbrances we are aware of on plain SAD block
					matching or single-direction SGM cost aggregation.
				</p>
				<p>
					The system conforms to the standard PAL (25 FPS interlaced) video timing on the
					capture side and the DE1-SoC's 640×480 VGA timing on the display side. No custom
					standards work was required.
				</p>
			</section>

