				</p>
				<p>
					We used generative AI tools to varying degrees in different parts of the project:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Block-matching RTL.</strong> We wrote the majority of the RTL ourselves
						and used generative AI (Claude Code, Microsoft Copilot) to identify or fix bugs.
						This was useful for catching subtle bugs (e.g. a shift register that did not
						actually shift the last index) and for trivial state-transition fixes spotted in
						the waveform.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Segmented memory + Avalon MMIO.</strong> We used generative AI to tweak
						the RTL to conform to the Avalon interface standard, and to write the TCL script
						that gets the module to appear as instantiable IP in the Qsys GUI.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Lens rectification RTL.</strong> Significantly AI-driven on both sides —
						the OpenCV code that extracts lens parameters from a set of images, and the RTL
						that explored several different implementations targeting different
						hardware-utilization points.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Software design-space exploration.</strong> AI was used heavily to try
						out different block-matching algorithms combined with different preprocessing
						and filtering steps without rewriting the harness for each.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Figures and animations for this report.</strong> We fed snippets of our
						RTL into AI to generate diagrams and the interactive block-matching animation
						embedded above.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					Our overall takeaway: <strong>generative AI was a productivity multiplier in some
					places and made bad ideas tractable in others.</strong> It was most effective for
					design-space exploration, where it let us get a sense of what kinds of results were
					achievable very quickly. It also accelerated RTL by catching bugs and writing
					testbenches.
				</p>
				<p>
					In other places, however, it produced overly complicated, poorly architected
					solutions we wouldn't have attempted to write ourselves. The clearest example is the
					rectification pipeline, which was significantly AI-generated and turned into the
					major performance bottleneck — the solution is correct, but it's incredibly hard to
					optimize or modify. By contrast, our block-matching RTL was almost entirely written
					by us (with AI used only to test and fix bugs), and that RTL is comparatively
					performant, easy to iterate on, and easy to understand conceptually. AI is
					impressive at producing correct RTL, but it tends to create solutions that are
					complex and difficult to iterate on without rigid guidance.
				</p>

